---
import BlogPostLayout from "../../layouts/BlogPostLayout.astro";
import BlogPhoto from "../../components/BlogPhoto.astro";
import { getBlogPost } from "../../data/blogPosts.js";

const post = getBlogPost("emotional-side-of-fatherhood");
---
<BlogPostLayout
  title={post.title}
  description={post.description}
  eyebrow="Mindset"
  dek={post.dek}
  date={post.date}
  readTime={post.readTime}
  image={post.image}
  isoMonth={post.isoMonth}
  faqs={post.faqs}
  ctaHeading="Hear the full conversation"
  ctaBody="We got into this one at length on the podcast — John and Mike, no scripts, on the stuff that's harder to say out loud than it is to write down."
  ctaHref="/podcast"
  ctaLabel="Listen to the episode"
>
  <p>
    Nobody warns you about the emotional side. Everyone will tell you about the sleep. Everyone
    has a sleep story. Nobody sits you down and tells you about the 2am doubt spiral — the one
    where you're staring at the ceiling wondering if you're actually going to be good at this, or
    just faking it well enough that no one notices yet.
  </p>

  <p>
    That feeling doesn't mean something's wrong with you. It means you care about doing this
    right. But if you don't do anything with it, it doesn't just sit there quietly — it turns into
    something else. A short temper. A bad mood you can't explain to your partner. Distance,
    right when you need to be closest.
  </p>

  <BlogPhoto src="/images/blog/quiet-moment.jpg" alt="A dad in a quiet, thoughtful moment outdoors" ratio="4/5" />

  <h2>The feeling of being useless in the room</h2>
  <p>
    This one hits hard in the first weeks. She's healing, she's feeding, she seems to just
    <em>know</em> things you don't — and you're standing there wondering what your actual job is.
    Here's the truth: your job in that moment isn't to have the answers. It's to be steady. Handing
    her water before she asks. Taking the baby for twenty minutes so she can shower without an
    audience. Being the person who doesn't need managing on top of everything else she's already
    managing.
  </p>
  <p>
    That's not a consolation prize. That's the job. It just doesn't feel like enough because it's
    quiet, and nobody claps for it.
  </p>

  <h2>The pressure to have it together when you don't</h2>
  <p>
    Somewhere along the way, a lot of us picked up the idea that a good dad doesn't struggle — he
    just handles it. That's not strength, that's just a mask, and masks take energy to hold up.
    Energy you don't have right now. The dads who actually come through this steady aren't the
    ones who never struggled. They're the ones who stopped pretending they weren't.
  </p>

  <div class="pull-quote">
    Name it out loud, to your partner or another dad, before it turns into a bad mood you can't
    explain. Sounds small. It isn't.
  </div>

  <h2>Why naming it out loud actually works</h2>
  <p>
    This is the one piece of advice that actually changed something for us. Not journaling about
    it, not white-knuckling through it — just saying the sentence out loud to another person.
    "I'm feeling like I don't know what I'm doing right now." That's it. That's the whole move.
  </p>
  <p>
    It works for two reasons. First, it takes the feeling out of your head, where it grows in
    silence, and puts it somewhere smaller — a sentence, spoken, that someone else can actually
    respond to. Second, it tells your partner the mood in the room isn't about her, which matters
    more than almost anything else you can do for the marriage in this stretch.
  </p>

  <BlogPhoto src="/images/blog/two-dads-talking.jpg" alt="Two dads talking over coffee at an outdoor table" ratio="16/9" />

  <h2>Who do you actually say it to</h2>
  <p>
    Your partner, when it's about the two of you. Another dad, when you just need someone who's
    been in it to say "yeah, that's normal" without flinching. That's a lot of what the Spit-Up
    Society ended up being for us and for the guys in it — a place to say the sentence out loud to
    people who won't make it weird.
  </p>
  <p>
    You don't need to have this figured out. You need one person you're willing to say the real
    sentence to, before the bad mood does the talking for you.
  </p>
</BlogPostLayout>
